# Build output paths and the size each may reach before the `builds` tool
# deletes it whole for the next build to refill (cargo mostly from sccache).
# scripts/build-layout.sh, run first by the hooks and every build script, links
# each path to /Volumes/Builds/bae/<checkout>/<path>, deletes the build copies
# in it that no build has used for three days (the tool's default), and then
# applies the caps. The hooks and build scripts run cargo through
# scripts/cargo.sh, which records each build copy cargo uses, rebuilt or up to
# date.
#
# The app checkouts (one per lane, mac and android) build without incremental
# compilation (the builds tool passes --config build.incremental=false there),
# and an `app <cap>` field is their cap. A `lane <lane>` field says that only that
# lane's app checkout builds into the folder: it stays warm and takes the app cap
# there, and is an ordinary side worktree's folder in the other lane's checkout.
# A folder with no lane (host `target`) is every app checkout's. Measured there on 2026-11-06 under load: a full macOS bridge
# build is 5.1G without incremental output against 8.0G with it (10.0G after
# one switch to another branch), the Android bridge 5.2G against 6.9G (8.1G);
# its host target held 7.5G with no incremental folders; its macOS .build holds
# runApp 2.8G, unsigned 3.2G and SourcePackages 3.2G. Its cargo caps are about
# twice those, its .build's about one and a half times, and an eviction there
# refills from sccache.
#
# Caps are about twice a fresh build of each: host 9.8G, macOS bridge 7.4G,
# Android 6.4G, iOS 12G; the macOS app's .build holds two Xcode build trees
# (scripts/xcode-trees.sh: runApp and unsigned) of 2.2G each fresh and the Swift
# packages both resolve into, 3.0G; the tool also deletes a tree on its own once
# no build has used it for three days; BaeKit's `swift build` 4.4G; BaeKit/Frameworks, the
# xcframework whose macOS and iOS slices build-macos.sh and build-ios.sh
# write, 1.4G and 1.9G; the others,
# the Android bridge's debug info among them (0.4G per ABI), are under 1G.
# target is about three times its fresh build: main's reached 26G across a day
# of clippy, tests and coven bumps, and a delete there loses the person's warm
# host build. target-ra is rust-analyzer's own: the Claude Code session's
# rust-analyzer (agent-instructions' rust-analyzer plugin) builds build scripts
# and proc macros there with `cargo check --workspace`; main's target held
# about 5G of check metadata and build-script output, older copies included.
#
# path                                cap  [app cap]  [lane]
target                                30G  app 15G
target-ra                             10G
target-macos                          15G  app 10G  lane mac
target-ios                            24G  lane mac
target-android                        13G  app 10G  lane android
shots                                 1G
bae-macos/bae/.build                  20G  app 14G  lane mac
# The app the person launches (main) and tests (the mac lane's app checkout) is
# built here; builds never ages it out in those two checkouts.
keep bae-macos/bae/.build/runApp
bae-ios/bae/.build                    10G  lane mac
BaeKit/.build                         9G   lane mac
BaeKit/Frameworks                     7G   lane mac
bae-android/.gradle                   1G   lane android
bae-android/debug-symbols             2G   lane android
bae-android/app/build                 2G   lane android
bae-android/bridge/build              1G   lane android
bae-android/fastvalues/build          1G   lane android
bae-android/screensubscription/build  1G   lane android
bae-avalonia/obj                      1G   lane mac
bae-avalonia/bin                      1G   lane mac
bae-avalonia/bae-avalonia.Tests/obj   1G   lane mac
bae-avalonia/bae-avalonia.Tests/bin   1G   lane mac
